# Mercury Pitch native build configuration.
# Copy to .env.local (gitignored) and fill in the values you need.
#
# RevenueCat public SDK keys ship inside the app binary by design, so these
# are not secrets. They are still per-store and per-environment, so keep the
# real ones out of Git and out of anyone else's build.
#
# V1-1 SELLS NOTHING. src/infrastructure/mobile-runtime.ts composes the runtime
# with no purchases options at all, so none of these reaches a store SDK yet.
# They are listed now because the build-time policy in
# @irchiinnuss/purchase-kit already reads them, and because the day one is
# needed is not the day to discover what it is called.

# Google Play key from RevenueCat -> Project settings -> API keys ("goog_...").
# Required for any build uploaded to Play once purchases are switched on.
VITE_REVENUECAT_ANDROID_KEY=

# App Store key ("appl_...").
VITE_REVENUECAT_IOS_KEY=

# Entitlement identifier, exactly as spelled in the RevenueCat dashboard.
VITE_REVENUECAT_ENTITLEMENT_ID=

# Set to 1 to mark this build as a debug artifact, which permits the RevenueCat
# Test Store. Needed because import.meta.env.DEV is false in every native build,
# so nothing else can tell a debug APK apart from a release one.
# NEVER set this for a build that goes to a store: the SDK aborts an app that
# configures a Test Store key in a release binary, and the build policy refuses
# it before that can happen.
VITE_REVENUECAT_ALLOW_TEST_STORE=

# Pin a specific offering instead of whichever one the dashboard marks
# current. Leave unset unless you are testing an alternative paywall.
VITE_REVENUECAT_OFFERING_ID=

# ── Google sign-in (checklist M-D1, M-D2) ───────────────────────────────
#
# THE REAL VALUES ARE IN `.env`, WHICH IS COMMITTED. They are public build
# constants, not secrets: an OAuth client id appears in every authorisation
# URL and ships inside the binary by design, and what protects the client is
# the bundle id plus the App Store signature on iOS and the package name plus
# the signing SHA-1 on Android. The client SECRET is a different string, it
# belongs to the worker, and it never appears in either file. They are listed
# here as well so this file stays a complete inventory of what the build
# reads — empty, because nothing here overrides `.env` except on purpose.
#
# Two ids, and they are NOT interchangeable:
#
#   VITE_GOOGLE_IOS_CLIENT_ID   the "iOS" client. Used by the Google SDK on
#                               iOS, and its REVERSED form is the URL scheme
#                               in ios/App/App/Info.plist (CFBundleURLTypes ->
#                               item 0 -> CFBundleURLSchemes -> item 0), so
#                               the two change together or neither changes.
#   VITE_GOOGLE_WEB_CLIENT_ID   the "Web application" client. Android's
#                               Credential Manager requires the WEB id here —
#                               passing the Android one is the single most
#                               common cause of "[28444] Developer console is
#                               not set up correctly". It is also the audience
#                               the db-worker verifies tokens against, so it
#                               must appear in the worker's GOOGLE_CLIENT_IDS.
#
# The Android OAuth client (package name + signing SHA-1) is registered in the
# Google Cloud console and is never pasted into this file or any other.
#
# Empty is still a supported state, and is what a build with neither id does:
# the key is omitted at initialize and the provider reports itself unavailable
# rather than failing halfway through a system sheet.
VITE_GOOGLE_IOS_CLIENT_ID=

VITE_GOOGLE_WEB_CLIENT_ID=
